#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124262 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124458 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd2b430e-fd5d-57ba-bc28-f7c113eda07a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Directional Derivative"}] :question/id #uuid "bf35c502-5a22-5953-9158-b2464d28c09f" :question/math-academy-id "e-3377" :question/problem "Find the directional derivative of the function $g(x, y) =3x^2+4xy$ at the point $A(0,1)$ in the direction towards the point $B(1,4).$" :question/worked-solution "The directional derivative $g'_{\\mathbf u}(a,b)$ of a function $g(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $\\mathbf v$ is given by\n\n$$\ng'_{\\mathbf u}(a,b) = \\nabla g(a,b)\\cdot\\mathbf u\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $\\mathbf v.$\n\nFirst, we find the direction vector and the corresponding unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨1,4⟩-⟨0,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,3⟩ \\\\ u & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}}{‖\\overset{\\to }{AB}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}}}⟨1,3⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟨1,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the gradient $\\nabla g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $A(0,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}+4xy),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}+4xy)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6x+4y,\\,4x⟩ \\\\ ∇g(0,1) & =⟨6\\cdot 0+4\\cdot 1,\\,4\\cdot 0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{u}^{′}(0,1) & =∇g(0,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨4,0⟩\\cdot ⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}},\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124263 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124459 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff351d3f-449b-5c41-8f72-d1917b2b7536" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Direction of Maximum Increase or Maximum Decrease of a Function"}] :question/id #uuid "dc1e4548-ce72-5618-86f4-b68b46b50550" :question/math-academy-id "e-3381" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $f (x, y) = \\ln (x^2y)$ decrease most rapidly at the point $(2, 1)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ decreases most rapidly in the direction opposite to that of its gradient.\n\nSo, we compute $-\\nabla f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-∇f(x,y) & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}y))\\text{ }i-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}y))\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}y}\\cdot 2xy\\text{ }i-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}y}\\cdot {x}^{2}j \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{x}\\text{ }i-\\frac{1}{y}\\text{ }j \\\\ -∇f(2,1) & =-\\frac{2}{2}\\text{ }i-\\frac{1}{1}\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function decreases most rapidly in the direction of $- \\, \\mathbf i - \\mathbf j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124264 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124460 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c330648f-69ad-5778-98cb-bd48a0b160dd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rate of Change of a Function in a Given Direction"}] :question/id #uuid "1eea4610-508a-5437-802e-9d009b777e82" :question/math-academy-id "e-3382" :question/problem "Consider the function $g (x, y) = \\ln \\left(\\sqrt {xy^3}\\right).$ What is the rate of change of $g$ at the point $\\left(\\dfrac 12, \\dfrac 32 \\right)$ in the direction in which the function increases most rapidly?" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $g(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nIn our case, we compute $\\nabla g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $\\left(\\dfrac 12, \\dfrac 32 \\right)\\!\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}})),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{3}}{2\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}},\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}}\\cdot \\frac{3x{y}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2x},\\,\\frac{3}{2y}⟩ \\\\ ∇g(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2}) & =⟨\\frac{1}{2\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2})},\\frac{3}{2\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2})}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $\\left(\\dfrac 12, \\dfrac 32 \\right)$ in the direction of $\\nabla g\\left(\\dfrac 12, \\dfrac 32 \\right)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖∇g(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2})‖ & =‖⟨1,1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265869 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124458 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd2b430e-fd5d-57ba-bc28-f7c113eda07a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Directional Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "086fecd5-a126-5f32-9308-1bc895c050b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263080} {:answer/id #uuid "8d43de9a-94e9-5878-939e-2733944f61c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264208} {:answer/id #uuid "9b78ab4c-27f6-5a87-8918-b6215e38243b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264329} {:answer/id #uuid "b92952ce-92d6-5fe1-84dc-4e5ec973c535" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264605} {:answer/id #uuid "cd1aafca-14b7-5d95-9816-988f20570192" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264795}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b78ab4c-27f6-5a87-8918-b6215e38243b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264329} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e1d9c83-156f-56e6-8ce6-3d01bf086064" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265433}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ae068a0a-3a23-5516-a4b7-13c70903e5db" :question/math-academy-id "q-130263" :question/problem "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-xy$ at the point $(1,1)$ in the direction of the vector $v=i-j.$" :question/worked-solution "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(i-j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-xy)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-xy)j \\\\  & =(2x-y)\\,i+(2y-x)\\,j \\\\ ∇\\,f(1,1) & =(2(1)-1)\\,i+(2(1)-1)\\,j \\\\  & =i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}(1,1) & =∇\\,f(1,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =(i+j)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(i-j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1(1)+1(-1)}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446242 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124458 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd2b430e-fd5d-57ba-bc28-f7c113eda07a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Directional Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "021bf1f8-b881-536a-a8f1-f59bbd10c0cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{18}{5\\sqrt{13}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446176} {:answer/id #uuid "455dead3-5a38-5f75-bb6f-3603611372d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446188} {:answer/id #uuid "502f0fe4-aa68-585a-bbd4-188d4c9372d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\sqrt{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446195} {:answer/id #uuid "5d9b9f76-b123-5719-bc4e-29ca3f63e30e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446197} {:answer/id #uuid "b8aca910-6177-50b0-976d-f5a0cad4d3f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446220}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "021bf1f8-b881-536a-a8f1-f59bbd10c0cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{18}{5\\sqrt{13}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446176} :answer-field/id #uuid "be838696-b1cd-5c8f-81ac-b0d86172dd65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446235}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5fc2a3e0-1de4-50ca-b644-c592b3edb577" :question/math-academy-id "q-130268" :question/problem "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ at the point $A(4,3)$ in the direction towards the point $B(1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the direction vector and the corresponding unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨1,1⟩-⟨4,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3,-2⟩ \\\\ u & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}}{‖\\overset{\\to }{AB}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+(-2)^{2}}}⟨-3,-2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}⟨-3,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $A(4,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}},\\,\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}⟩ \\\\ ∇f(4,3) & =⟨\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{3}^{2}}},\\,\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{3}^{2}}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{4}{5},\\frac{3}{5}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}(4,3) & =∇f(4,3)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{4}{5},\\frac{3}{5}⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}},-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{4(-3)+3(-2)}{5\\sqrt{13}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{18}{5\\sqrt{13}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446246 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124459 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff351d3f-449b-5c41-8f72-d1917b2b7536" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Direction of Maximum Increase or Maximum Decrease of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e31501d-4531-5b76-9877-d2a3acbb5d11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-6,1,-3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446178} {:answer/id #uuid "314f45e4-cf29-50b1-9428-b22c82951ca6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-2,-5,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446184} {:answer/id #uuid "4318aea0-1f2c-58b7-bdd1-6b19fbc76a15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,2,-4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446187} {:answer/id #uuid "46bb65c1-8132-59c6-9f4b-6909dff7bc8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,-1,4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446189} {:answer/id #uuid "f1d20d6e-c59b-5e2f-b608-e625a0868759" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-3,2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446229}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e31501d-4531-5b76-9877-d2a3acbb5d11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-6,1,-3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446178} :answer-field/id #uuid "8901f1a6-9dd3-5894-ab03-0ab176fa8fea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446234}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "beaf6ecd-5ac6-5efb-ad1b-41d64d5bbd40" :question/math-academy-id "q-130273" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $g(x,y,z)=10-3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-{z}^{3}$ increase most rapidly at the point $(1,-\\frac{1}{2},-1)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $g(x,y,z)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇g(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,-\\frac{1}{2},-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y,z) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(10-3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-{z}^{3}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(10-3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-{z}^{3}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂z}(10-3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-{z}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-6x,\\,-2y,\\,-3{z}^{2}⟩ \\\\ ∇g(1,-\\frac{1}{2},-1) & =⟨-6(1),-2(-\\frac{1}{2}),-3(-1)^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-6,1,-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $⟨-6,1,-3⟩.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446244 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124460 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c330648f-69ad-5778-98cb-bd48a0b160dd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rate of Change of a Function in a Given Direction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b6f9c9c-fd05-533b-ab92-794682300821" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446177} {:answer/id #uuid "19005152-b76d-5f3e-8e83-f9fa12abf908" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446181} {:answer/id #uuid "3b5f201e-9679-590f-9849-011ab1fefcb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446185} {:answer/id #uuid "60b225d0-997a-5fbc-a18b-ec3094252266" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446201} {:answer/id #uuid "86d2d586-ece9-5dd8-9965-84273626e130" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446209}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b6f9c9c-fd05-533b-ab92-794682300821" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446177} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a49be9b-1ed8-506c-ad9a-611025859067" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446231}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "754f0abc-16b3-51ff-9446-aa81fd4c05d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-130804" :question/problem "Consider the function $g(x,y)=4{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}.$ What is the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(2,1)$ in the direction of the vector $v=⟨5,12⟩?$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(2,1)$ in the direction of $v$ is equal to the directional derivative ${g}_{u}^{′}(2,1).$ \n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{12}^{2}}=13 \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{13}⟨5,12⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the gradient $∇g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8x,\\,-6y⟩ \\\\ ∇g(2,1) & =⟨8(2),-6(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨16,-6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the directional derivative ${g}_{u}^{′}(2,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{u}^{′}(2,1) & =∇g(2,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨16,-6⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{13}⟨5,12⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{16(5)-6(12)}{13} \\\\  & =\\frac{80-72}{13} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446245 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124460 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c330648f-69ad-5778-98cb-bd48a0b160dd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rate of Change of a Function in a Given Direction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40cd1b3b-3226-567f-8df7-b4203af0e8e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446186} {:answer/id #uuid "78161357-66de-58ad-955b-760220e2284c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446204} {:answer/id #uuid "7ced684c-4ebe-5a34-b02d-a8bda555a9f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446205} {:answer/id #uuid "7dfd9ca6-8542-5ff2-910b-fbe85fdf3f9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446207} {:answer/id #uuid "a3dd3ec0-5bd2-592f-a2b9-507da0e25254" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446214}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ced684c-4ebe-5a34-b02d-a8bda555a9f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446205} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6dc0783-a199-5ca8-ace2-ec2c865c730a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446238}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7d39de67-7fd2-5aeb-a063-e9cf738816af" :question/math-academy-id "q-130806" :question/problem "Consider the function $g(x,y)={x}^{y}.$ What is the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(1,1)$ in the direction in which the function increases most rapidly?" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $g(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nIn our case, we compute $∇g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{y}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{y})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨y{x}^{y-1},\\,{x}^{y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x⟩ \\\\ ∇\\,g(1,1) & =⟨(1){1}^{1-1},\\,({1}^{1})\\operatorname{ln}⁡1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(1,1)$ in the direction of $∇g(1,1)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖∇\\,g(1,1)‖ & =‖⟨1,0⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446243 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124460 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c330648f-69ad-5778-98cb-bd48a0b160dd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rate of Change of a Function in a Given Direction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4eb318ef-e5da-50bb-852d-abf0124a9413" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446194} {:answer/id #uuid "8d1128a0-3bc8-5ee6-8690-02b7875e3982" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446211} {:answer/id #uuid "94e3a4fd-f234-5419-b576-fa4fcdfcf2eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446213} {:answer/id #uuid "c41e1274-b58c-591c-ba2b-30404efa267c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446221} {:answer/id #uuid "c4350363-1f04-5bba-b31a-249136e6fc8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446222}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d1128a0-3bc8-5ee6-8690-02b7875e3982" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446211} :answer-field/id #uuid "716b4932-e73e-59f5-9ec5-4b4ed730dece" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446232}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "72e3df1a-6338-564c-adc2-85283b26953d" :question/math-academy-id "q-130811" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)=2\\sqrt{xy}.$ What is the rate of change of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(8,2)$ in the direction in which the function decreases most rapidly?" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ decreases most rapidly in the opposite direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nIn our case, we compute $-∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(8,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-∇f(x,y) & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2\\sqrt{xy})i-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2\\sqrt{xy})j \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{\\frac{y}{x}}\\,i-\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}}\\,j \\\\ -∇f(8,2) & =-\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{8}}\\,i-\\sqrt{\\frac{8}{2}}\\,j \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\,i-2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the rate of change of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(8,2)$ in the direction of $-∇f(8,2)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖-∇f(8,2)‖ & =‖-\\frac{1}{2}\\,i-2\\,j‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{17}{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124962 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124458 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd2b430e-fd5d-57ba-bc28-f7c113eda07a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Directional Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91e3020e-b220-554e-a3f0-07e483342e90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\sqrt{21}}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122373} {:answer/id #uuid "a1e6986e-ec79-5752-9e0d-ea5960beab16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14\\sqrt{29}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122374} {:answer/id #uuid "a8340c19-e527-5c1b-953c-c260766408b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14\\sqrt{21}}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122375} {:answer/id #uuid "db0760d0-b134-5ae3-af68-54c39c2f1781" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10\\sqrt{29}}{29}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122376} {:answer/id #uuid "0801896c-1c2b-510b-86df-c0542a7bf4e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14\\sqrt{29}}{29}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122377}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0801896c-1c2b-510b-86df-c0542a7bf4e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14\\sqrt{29}}{29}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122377} :answer-field/id #uuid "5dfb4078-c321-5580-bb20-9356ac667745" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122372}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4ba373e3-c7d5-54ce-a51c-b2950c874b69" :question/math-academy-id "q-36273" :question/problem "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=2x+{y}^{2}$ at the point $(0,1)$ in the direction of the vector $v=5i+2j.$" :question/worked-solution "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{2}^{2}}=\\sqrt{29} \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{29}}(5i+2j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x+{y}^{2})\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+{y}^{2})\\text{ }j \\\\  & =2\\,i+2y\\,j \\\\ ∇\\,f(0,1) & =2\\,i+(2\\cdot 1)\\,j \\\\  & =2i+2j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}(0,1) & =∇\\,f(0,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =(2i+2j)\\cdot (\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{29}}i+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{29}}j) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 5+2\\cdot 2}{\\sqrt{29}} \\\\  & =\\frac{14\\sqrt{29}}{29}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124963 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124459 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff351d3f-449b-5c41-8f72-d1917b2b7536" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Direction of Maximum Increase or Maximum Decrease of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "476511f4-e638-543b-8280-a5b7db9ba896" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1)-2)\\,i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446190} {:answer/id #uuid "5ebdb20c-42b9-5f29-a21e-e6adefd06cd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1)\\,i+2j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446199} {:answer/id #uuid "6d736c41-9b37-5824-a068-b4c8dfbf00b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2i+j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446203} {:answer/id #uuid "7d2a5ab5-1e1e-50a2-abb5-df9207b0da19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1))\\,i+j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446206} {:answer/id #uuid "b601e5c8-a43b-53e7-ba27-d8c6515bae90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446219}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d2a5ab5-1e1e-50a2-abb5-df9207b0da19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1))\\,i+j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446206} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e3d8181-5a0a-57e3-b64b-0cfbd5cc5890" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122378}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e75dd59c-9b9f-50d9-a9a4-c1850c66ee84" :question/math-academy-id "q-36293" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{x}^{2}y$ increase most rapidly at the point $(1,1)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{x}^{2}y)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{x}^{2}y)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(2xy-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\text{ }i+{x}^{2}\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(1,1) & =(2\\cdot 1\\cdot 1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1))\\text{ }i+{1}^{2}\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(2-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1))\\text{ }i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $(2-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1))\\,i+j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446241 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124458 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd2b430e-fd5d-57ba-bc28-f7c113eda07a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Directional Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d2f5038-f42a-5fd7-a95d-a297da774c15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446193} {:answer/id #uuid "a9d07651-85d5-5624-ae13-7a41e816c585" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446216} {:answer/id #uuid "b248e7e5-413b-5466-b7ed-293308db0e18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446218} {:answer/id #uuid "d058285d-919c-56d4-bc37-2e175c0f050a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446225} {:answer/id #uuid "eb4ac023-e663-5e98-b2d3-aa3c2ec67064" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446228}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d058285d-919c-56d4-bc37-2e175c0f050a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446225} :answer-field/id #uuid "cea3cee4-a045-5dc7-8596-6cf26a0cb161" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446236}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4c3374df-275b-5fb9-8864-40efeec8b5b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-36306" :question/problem "Given the function  $g(x,y)=({x}^{2}+y)^{2}$, find its directional derivative at the\npoint $A(-1,1)$ in the direction that's towards the point $B(3,4).$" :question/worked-solution "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the direction vector and the corresponding unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨3,4⟩-⟨-1,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,3⟩ \\\\ u & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}}{‖\\overset{\\to }{AB}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}}}⟨4,3⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨4,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the gradient $∇g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $A(-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+y)^{2},\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+y)^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4x({x}^{2}+y),\\,2({x}^{2}+y)⟩ \\\\ ∇g(-1,1) & =⟨4\\cdot (-1)\\cdot ((-1)^{2}+1),2\\cdot ((-1)^{2}+1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-8,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{u}^{′}(-1,1) & =∇g(-1,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨-8,4⟩\\cdot ⟨\\frac{4}{5},\\frac{3}{5}⟩ \\\\  & =-8\\cdot \\frac{4}{5}+4\\cdot \\frac{3}{5} \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125028 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124460 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c330648f-69ad-5778-98cb-bd48a0b160dd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rate of Change of a Function in a Given Direction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10d46f12-0b75-50c9-a911-0b663e2a7dd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi +e}}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446179} {:answer/id #uuid "1683b5bd-9e3c-568d-b68d-d3cd665c9d95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{{\\pi }^{4}+{e}^{2}}}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446180} {:answer/id #uuid "5e6ee910-194a-5db1-a61b-cb7e82e92ef2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{{\\pi }^{2}+{e}^{2}}}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446198} {:answer/id #uuid "7f7aff75-c59d-50b0-ace5-77c397f97e98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{{\\pi }^{2}+{e}^{2}}}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446208} {:answer/id #uuid "cfe40dc8-4b41-5a6b-bd2a-d1a558dc2365" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{\\pi +e}{e}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446224}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e6ee910-194a-5db1-a61b-cb7e82e92ef2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{{\\pi }^{2}+{e}^{2}}}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446198} :answer-field/id #uuid "4aca3088-e8f0-5dbc-95a5-523c9e4320d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122768}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8ea3ebc5-2c81-5696-aab4-931973a19233" :question/math-academy-id "q-37074" :question/problem "Consider the function $g(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x).$ What is the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(e,\\pi )$ in the direction in which the function increases most rapidly?" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $g(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nIn our case, we compute $∇g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(e,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\cdot \\frac{y}{x},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡x⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{x},\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)⟩ \\\\ ∇\\,g(e,\\pi ) & =⟨\\frac{\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\operatorname{ln}⁡e)}{e},\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡e\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\operatorname{ln}⁡e)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi }{e},\\,1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\pi \\cdot (-1)}{e},\\,1\\cdot (-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{\\pi }{e},\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(e,\\pi )$ in the direction of $∇g(e,\\pi )$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖∇\\,g(e,\\pi )‖ & =‖⟨-\\frac{\\pi }{e},-1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{{e}^{2}}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{\\pi }^{2}+{e}^{2}}}{e}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351517 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124459 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff351d3f-449b-5c41-8f72-d1917b2b7536" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Direction of Maximum Increase or Maximum Decrease of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e2d1818-23b6-5bc6-9705-1aa1f8fcde6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({e}^{-1}+1)\\text{ }i+({e}^{-1}-1)j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349013} {:answer/id #uuid "68eb9247-b932-5869-944e-6c106bd7cb39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(e-1)\\text{ }i+ej" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349413} {:answer/id #uuid "6fd17f28-20e2-5ce1-8906-d302e5e5f909" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({e}^{-1}+1)\\text{ }i+{e}^{-1}j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349472} {:answer/id #uuid "91bd11c8-e407-5439-bdc4-1470bfd9ed1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({e}^{-1}-1)\\text{ }i+{e}^{-1}j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349749} {:answer/id #uuid "df06fa9d-9784-5210-b854-98e07a5502ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(e+1)\\text{ }i+(e-1)j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350399}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91bd11c8-e407-5439-bdc4-1470bfd9ed1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({e}^{-1}-1)\\text{ }i+{e}^{-1}j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349749} :answer-field/id #uuid "49ce0ac9-9872-520a-b868-69cd398be0c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350832}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4e724341-d4b7-5d33-9112-e9d06eaccd62" :question/math-academy-id "q-83554" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+y{e}^{x}-xy$ increase most rapidly at the point $(-1,1)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+y{e}^{x}-xy)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+y{e}^{x}-xy)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+y{e}^{x}-y)\\text{ }i+(\\frac{x}{y}+{e}^{x}-x)\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(1,1) & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+1\\cdot {e}^{-1}-1)\\text{ }i+(\\frac{-1}{1}+{e}^{-1}-(-1))\\text{ }j \\\\  & =({e}^{-1}-1)\\text{ }i+{e}^{-1}j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $({e}^{-1}-1)\\text{ }i+{e}^{-1}j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446240 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124459 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff351d3f-449b-5c41-8f72-d1917b2b7536" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Direction of Maximum Increase or Maximum Decrease of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a640dc4-f0d6-5221-9834-4d0e7ba9f870" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-3,\\,-4,\\,4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446191} {:answer/id #uuid "8b8bfd4d-1ee5-507f-b0d0-bf0d05bf14af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,\\,-2,\\,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446210} {:answer/id #uuid "8e8a41f1-5149-587a-ba0e-8dfbff363aab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨3,\\,4,\\,-4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446212} {:answer/id #uuid "a78d148d-8a70-55a2-a873-68980ae2bd0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,\\,2,\\,-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446215} {:answer/id #uuid "b0d6b2ea-bb17-5bc1-89b4-f847433baa23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,-2,\\,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446217}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b8bfd4d-1ee5-507f-b0d0-bf0d05bf14af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,\\,-2,\\,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446210} :answer-field/id #uuid "da37f824-4e73-5b4a-a18f-250ab74de323" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446237}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3642d7fd-0113-5cbf-a61e-17061219f8d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-83598" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $g(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+z)$ decrease most rapidly at the point $(1,1,1)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $g(x,y,z)$ decreases most rapidly in the direction opposite to that of its gradient.\n\nSo, we compute $-∇g(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-∇g(x,y,z) & =-⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+z)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+z)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{y}^{2}+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+z))⟩ \\\\  & =-⟨{y}^{2}-\\frac{4}{x+z},\\,2xy,\\,-\\frac{4}{x+z}⟩ \\\\ -∇g(1,1,1) & =-⟨{1}^{2}-\\frac{4}{1+1},\\,2\\cdot 1\\cdot 1,\\,-\\frac{4}{1+1}⟩ \\\\  & =-⟨-1,\\,2,\\,-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-2,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function decreases most rapidly in the direction of $⟨1,\\,-2,\\,2⟩.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446239 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124460 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c330648f-69ad-5778-98cb-bd48a0b160dd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rate of Change of a Function in a Given Direction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e7ee258-aeb5-5bdf-92b6-350be2acf019" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{12}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446182} {:answer/id #uuid "2f930ebf-9d00-57ce-9436-e66479caf16c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446183} {:answer/id #uuid "5b48cb68-dd75-5ec3-b835-523ae565ed53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446196} {:answer/id #uuid "d6b0abaf-e4dd-5cab-82a0-5a8354af1953" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446226} {:answer/id #uuid "de3f161a-4d79-5e42-a5c8-9b89385ad3f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{16}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446227}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de3f161a-4d79-5e42-a5c8-9b89385ad3f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{16}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446227} :answer-field/id #uuid "33218d7b-e61f-5da1-bb1d-d60915785e82" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446230}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "30b363a1-05a8-5997-8feb-6bd8aaa30fa0" :question/math-academy-id "q-83609" :question/problem "Consider the function $g(x,y)=xy{e}^{x+y}+{x}^{2}y.$ What is the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(1,-1)$ in the direction of the vector $v=⟨3,-4⟩?$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(1,-1)$ in the direction of $v$ is equal to the directional derivative ${g}_{u}^{′}(1,-1).$ \n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(-4)^{2}}=\\sqrt{25}=5 \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨3,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the gradient $∇\\,g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy{e}^{x+y}+{x}^{2}y),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy{e}^{x+y}+{x}^{2}y)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨y{e}^{x+y}+xy{e}^{x+y}+2xy,\\,x{e}^{x+y}+xy{e}^{x+y}+{x}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨y(1+x){e}^{x+y}+2xy,\\,x(1+y){e}^{x+y}+{x}^{2}⟩ \\\\ ∇\\,g(1,-1) & =⟨(-1)\\cdot (1+1){e}^{1-1}+2\\cdot 1\\cdot (-1),\\,1\\cdot (1-1){e}^{1-1}+{1}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the directional derivative ${g}_{u}^{′}(1,-1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{u}^{′}(1,-1) & =∇\\,g(1,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨-4,1⟩\\cdot ⟨\\frac{3}{5},-\\frac{4}{5}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{(-4)\\cdot 3+1\\cdot (-4)}{5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{16}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354470 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124458 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd2b430e-fd5d-57ba-bc28-f7c113eda07a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Directional Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1304514c-b435-522f-ad3a-591f2559a22b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11\\sqrt{34}}{34}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351730} {:answer/id #uuid "7e4fba7a-fe7c-5a9c-9fbe-87782d23df70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11\\sqrt{43}}{43}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352676} {:answer/id #uuid "c8a1b502-7fbf-5960-aaec-5f60565ea22e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10\\sqrt{34}}{34}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353314} {:answer/id #uuid "c94460ac-494e-513e-82ed-c599cb03d029" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10\\sqrt{43}}{34}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353320} {:answer/id #uuid "f1e9e528-e824-57bb-899a-9e75210a8db6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{34}}{34}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353676}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8a1b502-7fbf-5960-aaec-5f60565ea22e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10\\sqrt{34}}{34}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353314} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9f8833b-3f19-5c49-a196-1b1b54521800" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354216}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f8f45df0-069c-5e4a-a4d7-bdd2e15f5467" :question/math-academy-id "q-86429" :question/problem "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=6{x}^{2}+2y$ at the point $(0,1)$ in the direction of the vector $v=3i+5j.$" :question/worked-solution "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{5}^{2}}=\\sqrt{34} \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{34}}(3i+5j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6{x}^{2}+2y)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(6{x}^{2}+2y)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =12x\\,i+2\\,j \\\\ ∇\\,f(0,1) & =(12\\cdot 0)\\,i+2\\,j \\\\  & =2j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}(0,1) & =∇\\,f(0,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =2j\\cdot (\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{34}}i+\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{34}}j) \\\\  & =\\frac{0\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 5}{\\sqrt{34}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\sqrt{34}}{34}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446247 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124459 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff351d3f-449b-5c41-8f72-d1917b2b7536" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Direction of Maximum Increase or Maximum Decrease of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01fea9f5-7ff4-5f0d-b619-5f87a6a90c35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2{e}^{4},\\,2{e}^{2}+3,\\,12⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446175} {:answer/id #uuid "4cc668bd-9c56-5b86-a394-e8a7b8069688" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2e,\\,{e}^{4}-3,\\,12⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446192} {:answer/id #uuid "604a5840-27db-5147-b4e6-7928a771885b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4{e}^{2},\\,2{e}^{4}-3,\\,-12⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446200} {:answer/id #uuid "682d87e6-5a4b-538f-8f34-c5cd1f0d46e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2{e}^{4},\\,2{e}^{4}+3,\\,-12⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446202} {:answer/id #uuid "c9528b5a-8771-5176-a89b-c59ec1b7b7ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2{e}^{4},\\,2e-3,\\,-21⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446223}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "682d87e6-5a4b-538f-8f34-c5cd1f0d46e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2{e}^{4},\\,2{e}^{4}+3,\\,-12⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446202} :answer-field/id #uuid "845bc3ce-d9e8-5c27-8b7e-c01d5d7a2ced" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446233}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f9974a87-21df-58c9-86ba-41826947b876" :question/math-academy-id "q-86539" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $g(x,y,z)={e}^{xy}+3{z}^{2}y$ increase most rapidly at the point $(2,2,-1)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $g(x,y,z)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇g(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,2,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y,z) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{xy}+3{z}^{2}y),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{xy}+3{z}^{2}y),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂z}({e}^{xy}+3{z}^{2}y)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨y{e}^{xy},\\,x{e}^{xy}+3{z}^{2},\\,6yz⟩ \\\\ ∇g(2,2,-1) & =⟨2\\cdot {e}^{2\\cdot 2},\\,2\\cdot {e}^{2\\cdot 2}+3(-1)^{2},\\,6\\cdot 2\\cdot (-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{e}^{4},\\,2{e}^{4}+3,\\,-12⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $⟨2{e}^{4},\\,2{e}^{4}+3,\\,-12⟩.$"}]}
